    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q3a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the diagonals are equal.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$AC=BD=2\\sqrt{26}$, so the diagonals are equal.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}AC&=\\sqrt{(0-2)^2+(7-(-3))^2}=\\sqrt{104}=2\\sqrt{26}\\\\BD&=\\sqrt{(-4-6)^2+(1-3)^2}=\\sqrt{104}=2\\sqrt{26}\\end{align*}$$\nThe diagonals are equal.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q3b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that its diagonals meet at right angles.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$m_{AC}=-5$ and $m_{BD}=\\frac15$. Their product is $-1$, so the diagonals meet at right angles.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{AC}&=\\frac{7-(-3)}{0-2}=-5\\\\m_{BD}&=\\frac{1-3}{-4-6}=\\frac15\\\\m_{AC}\\times m_{BD}&=-5\\times\\frac15=-1\\end{align*}$$\nThe diagonals meet at right angles.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q3c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that the diagonals bisect each other.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both diagonals have midpoint $(1,2)$, so they bisect each other.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Midpoint of }AC&=\\left(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{-3+7}{2}\\right)=(1,2)\\\\\\text{Midpoint of }BD&=\\left(\\frac{6+(-4)}{2},\\frac{3+1}{2}\\right)=(1,2)\\end{align*}$$\nThe diagonals have the same midpoint, so they bisect each other.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q3d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Hence explain why $ABCD$ must be a square.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Its equal diagonals bisect each other at right angles, so $ABCD$ is a square.",
          "worked": "$ABCD$ has equal diagonals that bisect each other at right angles, so $ABCD$ is a square.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 15
      }
    ]
  },
